ter !important;\n}\n\n.contact-widget .widget-body p {\n  color: rgba(255, 255, 255, .8) !important;\n}\n\n.contact-num {\n  font-size: 20px !important;\n  font-weight: 700 !important;\n  color: var(--gold) !important;\n  letter-spacing: .02em !important;\n  margin: 8px 0 !important;\n}\n\n.contact-hours {\n  font-size: 12px !important;\n  color: rgba(255, 255, 255, .6) !important;\n  margin-bottom: 16px !important;\n  line-height: 1.6 !important;\n}\n\n/* Footer */\n.site-footer {\n  background: var(--navy) !important;\n  color: rgba(255, 255, 255, .6) !important;\n  text-align: center !important;\n  padding: 22px !important;\n  font-size: 13px !important;\n}\n\n.site-footer a {\n  color: rgba(255, 255, 255, .6) !important;\n}\n\n/* Responsive */\n@media (max-width: 900px) {\n  .content-wrap {\n    grid-template-columns: 1fr !important;\n  }\n  .sidebar {\n    position: static !important;\n  }\n}\n\n@media (max-width: 600px) {\n  .container {\n    padding: 0 16px !important;\n  }\n  .header-cta .btn-outline {\n    display: none !important;\n  }\n  .article-hero {\n    padding: 38px 0 48px !important;\n  }\n  .cta-box {\n    padding: 22px !important;\n  }\n  .author-box {\n    flex-direction: column !important;\n  }\n}\n</style>\n<header class=\"site-header\"><div class=\"container\"><div class=\"header-inner\">\n  <a href=\"https://drshivdiggikar.com/\" class=\"logo\">\n    <div class=\"logo-mark\">⚕</div>\n    <div><div class=\"logo-name\">Dr. Shivashankar Diggikar</div><div class=\"logo-sub\">FRCPCH · FRCPI · Neonatologist &amp; Pediatrician</div></div>\n  </a>\n  <div class=\"header-cta\">\n    <a href=\"https://drshivdiggikar.com/blog/\" class=\"btn btn-outline\">← All Articles</a>\n    <a href=\"https://www.medicoverhospitals.in/doctors/dr-shivashankar-diggikar\" class=\"btn btn-primary\">Book Appointment</a>\n  </div>\n</div></div></header>\n<section class=\"article-hero\"><div class=\"container\"><div class=\"hero-inner\">\n  <div class=\"breadcrumb\"><a href=\"https://drshivdiggikar.com/\">Home</a><span>›</span><a href=\"https://drshivdiggikar.com/blog/\">Blog</a><span>›</span><span>Newborn Jaundice Bangalore — Causes, Signs & When to Act</span></div>\n  <div class=\"category-badge\">🏥 Neonatology · MOFU</div>\n  <h1 class=\"article-title\">Newborn Jaundice Bangalore — Causes, Signs & When to Act</h1>\n  <div class=\"article-meta\">\n    <span class=\"meta-item\">👨‍⚕️ Dr. Shivashankar Diggikar, FRCPCH</span>\n    <span class=\"meta-dot\"></span><span class=\"meta-item\">📅 2025</span>\n    <span class=\"meta-dot\"></span><span class=\"meta-item\">⏱ 8 min read</span>\n    <span class=\"meta-dot\"></span><span class=\"meta-item\">📍 Medicover Whitefield, Bangalore</span>\n  </div>\n</div></div></section>\n<div class=\"container\"><div class=\"content-wrap\">\n<article class=\"article-body\">\n\n<p>Jaundice in newborns is one of the most common reasons a family ends up back at the hospital in the first week of life. While jaundice is often harmless and resolves on its own, there are specific situations where it requires <strong>prompt medical attention</strong>.</p>\n\n<h2>What Causes Jaundice in Newborns?</h2>\n<p>Jaundice occurs when <strong>bilirubin</strong> — a yellow pigment produced by the breakdown of red blood cells — builds up in the blood faster than the baby's liver can process it.</p>\n\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n  <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>When It Appears</th><th>Cause</th><th>Concern Level</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>Physiological</td><td>Day 2–3</td><td>Normal newborn transition</td><td>Usually low</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Breastfeeding jaundice</td><td>First week</td><td>Insufficient milk intake</td><td>Low–moderate</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Breast milk jaundice</td><td>After day 5–7</td><td>Substance in breast milk</td><td>Usually mild</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Haemolytic jaundice</td><td>Within 24 hrs</td><td>Blood group incompatibility</td><td>HIGH — urgent</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Premature baby jaundice</td><td>Earlier, more severe</td><td>Immature liver</td><td>Moderate–high</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table></div>\n\n<h2>Signs of Newborn Jaundice — What to Look For</h2>\n<p>Jaundice progresses in a predictable pattern — it starts on the <strong>face and forehead</strong> and spreads downwards. A useful rule: the further down the body the yellow colour has spread, the higher the bilirubin level is likely to be.</p>\n<p>To check: press gently on the skin with a finger. When you release, if the area looks yellow, jaundice is likely. In darker-skinned babies, look at the <strong>whites of the eyes</strong> and inside the mouth — more reliable indicators.</p>\n\n<div class=\"warning-box\">\n  <div class=\"warning-box-title\">⚠️ When Is Jaundice an Emergency? — See a Doctor Immediately</div>\n  <ul style=\"margin:0;font-size:15px\">\n    <li>Jaundice appears within the <strong>first 24 hours</strong> of life</li>\n    <li>Yellow discolouration spreads rapidly to the abdomen, legs, and feet</li>\n    <li>Baby is extremely sleepy and difficult to wake for feeds</li>\n    <li>Baby is arching backwards or has a high-pitched, unusual cry</li>\n    <li>Stools are pale/white or urine is dark yellow/orange</li>\n    <li>Jaundice persists beyond 3 weeks in a full-term baby</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>How Is Newborn Jaundice Treated?</h2>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Phototherapy (Light Treatment)</strong> — Blue-spectrum LED lights break down bilirubin in the skin. Painless, effective, and completely safe. The baby's eyes are covered with small protective patches.</li>\n  <li><strong>Enhanced Breastfeeding</strong> — Increasing feeding frequency to 10–12 times in 24 hours often resolves breastfeeding jaundice without phototherapy.</li>\n  <li><strong>Intravenous Immunoglobulin (IVIG)</strong> — Used specifically in haemolytic jaundice (blood group incompatibility).</li>\n  <li><strong>Exchange Transfusion</strong> — Reserved for very severe cases. The baby's blood is gradually replaced with donor blood to rapidly lower bilirubin.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Can Jaundice Cause Brain Damage?</h2>\n<p>Untreated <em>severe</em> jaundice can cross into the brain and cause <strong>kernicterus</strong> — a condition causing permanent neurological damage. However, this outcome is very rare in a setting where jaundice is properly monitored and treated. When identified and treated early, the risk of brain damage is essentially eliminated.</p>\n<blockquote>\"The purpose of phototherapy is precisely to prevent bilirubin from ever reaching damaging levels. Early identification is everything.\" — Dr. Diggikar</blockquote>\n\n<div class=\"cta-box\">\n  <h3>📞 Book a Consultation with Dr. Diggikar</h3>\n  <p>Concerned about your child's health? Dr. Shivashankar Diggikar FRCPCH is Bangalore's leading neonatologist &amp; pediatrician at Medicover Whitefield.</p>\n  <a href=\"https://www.medicoverhospitals.in/doctors/dr-shivashankar-diggikar\" class=\"btn btn-white\">Book Online →</a>\n  <div class=\"cta-meta\">\n    <span>📞 +91-9133555335</span>\n    <span>📍 Medicover Whitefield, Bangalore</span>\n    <span>🕐 Mon–Sat: 9:30 AM–2:30 PM</span>\n  </div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"author-box\">\n  <div class=\"author-avatar\">👨‍⚕️</div>\n  <div>\n    <div class=\"author-name\">Dr. Shivashankar Diggikar</div>\n    <div class=\"author-creds\">FRCPCH (UK) · FRCPI (Ireland) · BMJ Editor · 30+ Publications</div>\n    <div class=\"author-bio\">Director of Neonatal-Perinatal Services at Medicover Women &amp; Children Hospital, Whitefield, Bangalore. London-trained neonatologist with 15+ years of NICU and paediatric experience. Consults at Medicover Whitefield (Mon–Sat, 9:30 AM–2:30 PM) and Oyster Mother &amp; Child Hospital, Banaswadi (Mon–Sat, 5:30–7:00 PM).</div>\n  </div>\n</div>\n</article>\n<aside class=\"sidebar\">\n  <div class=\"widget contact-widget\">\n    <div class=\"widget-head\">Book a Consultation</div>\n    <div class=\"widget-body\">\n      <p>Neonatologist &amp; Pediatrician<br>Medicover Whitefield</p>\n      <div class=\"contact-num\">+91-9133555335</div>\n      <div class=\"contact-hours\">Mon–Sat: 9:30 AM – 2:30 PM<br>Eve: 5:30 PM – 7:00 PM</div>\n      <a href=\"https://www.medicoverhospitals.in/doctors/dr-shivashankar-diggikar\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" style=\"width:100%;justify-content:center\">Book Appointment</a>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"widget\"><div class=\"widget-head\">Quick Facts</div><div class=\"widget-body\"><ul class=\"quick-facts\"><li>Jaundice appears day 2–3 (physiological)</li><li>Jaundice in first 24 hrs = always urgent</li><li>Phototherapy is safe and effective</li><li>Breast milk reduces risk of NEC</li><li>Kernicterus is preventable with early treatment</li></ul></div></div>\n  <div class=\"widget\"><div class=\"widget-head\">Related Articles</div><div class=\"widget-body\" style=\"padding:0\"><ul class=\"related-list\"><li><a href=\"#\">What Happens in a NICU?</a></li><li><a href=\"#\">Premature Baby Care: 27–32 Weeks</a></li><li><a href=\"#\">Your Baby's First 30 Days</a></li><li><a href=\"#\">Vaccination Schedule 0–2 Years</a></li><li><a href=\"#\">Choosing a Pediatrician in Whitefield</a></li></ul></div></div>\n</aside>\n</div></div>\n<footer class=\"site-footer\"><div class=\"container\"><p>© 2025 Dr. Shivashankar Diggikar · FRCPCH (UK) · FRCPI (Ireland) · Medicover Whitefield, Bangalore · <a href=\"tel:+919133555335\">+91-9133555335</a></p></div></footer>"
